\endomit

    \section1 Implementation Details

    Everything in the application has been designed in a modular fashion. Main view, which is
    the \c {Main.qml} file, is an \l {ApplicationWindow}, which instantiates the data simulators
    for each graph, as well as the root \l Item holding all of the UI elements.

    The \l {FontLoader} is used for applying a global font for the whole UI.

    \qml
    ApplicationWindow {
        ...

        FontLoader { ... }
        font.family: interFont.name

        // Data simulators
        PieSimulator {
            id: piesim
            ...
        }

        BarSimulator {
            id: barsim
            ...
        }

        LineSimulator {
            id: linesim
            ...
        }

        Data3DSimulator {
            id: barDataSimulator
            ...
        }

        Data3DSimulator {
            id: scatterDataSimulator
            ...
        }

        Data3DSimulator {
            id: surfaceDataSimulator
            ...
        }

        // Root item
        Item {...}
    }
    \endqml

    The root item holds the background graphics and a \l Layout handling the placement of
    each UI element in the view:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/Main.qml rootitem
    \dots

    Most of the elements have been taken out of the \c {Main.qml} file itself into their own
    QML components to be used in the layouts, like the \c ToolBarItem in the snippet below:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/Main.qml toolbar
    \dots

    Transitions between different view is handled using \c states and \c {transitions}:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/Main.qml states
    \dots

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/Main.qml transitions

    \section2 Dashboard

    Dashboard is a part of the main content, another \l RowLayout inside the \l Layout hierarchy:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/Main.qml maincontent
    \dots

    It is made up of a status pane and a dashboard pane:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/Main.qml dashboard

    \section3 Status Pane

    Status pane has also been made up of smaller elements, implemented in their own QML
    components:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/StatusPane.qml statuspane
    \dots

    The implementation of these is not relevant, so that part will be skipped.

    \section3 Dashboard Pane

    Dashboard is the element holding the six small graph previews. Only four of those are
    visible at a time, so they have to be added inside an element that can be scrolled.
    In this case, it is done with a \l {Flickable}, which holds a \l {GridLayout}:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/DashboardPane.qml dashboard
    \dots

    The graph previews are added inside the grid layout, and like most other elements,
    they have been implemented inside their own elements:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/DashboardPane.qml previews
    \dots 0

    The \c SmallGraphItem element is a generic one, holding all different graph types
    inside it, and the required type is given to it as a \c {state}.

    SmallGraphItem has:
    \list
    \li A header element
    \li An instance of each graph type, for which visibility is set to false by default using an
        opacity trigger
    \li A rectangle to darken the preview
    \li A mouse area to handle hover and mouse clicks on the preview
    \endlist

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/SmallGraphItem.qml previewgraphs
    \dots 0

    Opacities are used for transition animations, which is why they have been set to 0 in the
    previews by default. When opacity is animated above 0.5, the preview graph itself is set
    visible:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/Bar3DGraph.qml bargraph
    \dots

    \section2 Details

    Details is also a part of the main content, using the same \l RowLayout inside the
    \l Layout hierarchy. It is made up of the same status pane as the \l {Dashboard}, and
    a details pane:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/Main.qml detail

    The details pane, \c {BigGraphItem}, is similar to the \c SmallGraphItem in a sense that
    it also holds an instance of each graph in it, in addition to other elements:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/BigGraphItem.qml graphs
    \dots 0

    Other elements included are a dropdown combobox for selecting the graph type, control
    buttons for rotating, panning, and zooming - depending on the graph type - and  a tooltip
    for the buttons. Like \c {SmallGraphItem}, the graph type shown is decided based on
    the state.

    \section2 Settings

    Settings has been taken outside the main content \l {Layout}, as it is not using any parts
    of the \l Dashboard or \l Details views:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/Main.qml settings
    \dots

    Settings view holds another \c BigGraphItem for displaying the graph, and a settings pane
    for adjusting either the \l GraphsTheme properties or some properties sepcific to the
    selected graph type.

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/SettingsItem.qml settingsview
    \dots

    \section3 Global Theme

    The settings on the \uicontrol {General Settings} tab affect all graphs in the application.
    A global \l GraphsTheme has been implemented as a \c Singleton in \c {GlobalSettings.qml}:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/GlobalSettings.qml singleton
    \dots 0
    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/GlobalSettings.qml global
    \dots
    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/GlobalSettings.qml sharedtheme

    This global shared theme is set to each graph as the theme:

    \snippet common/aerospacehub/qml/Area2DGraph.qml theme

    As a singleton, the \c GlobalSettings.qml needs to be tagged as one in the \c {CMakeLists.txt}
    as well, to be usable in the application:

    \code
    set_source_files_properties(qml/GlobalSettings.qml
        PROPERTIES QT_QML_SINGLETON_TYPE TRUE)
    \endcode

    \section3 Graph Settings

    The settings in the \uicontrol {Graph Settings} tab only affect the currently selected
    graph. These settings do not change the global theme, but the series in the active
    graph only.
*/
